    "answer": {
      "text": "A total solar eclipse happens because the Moon passes directly between the Earth and the Sun, aligning them in a straight line. This alignment is necessary because the Moon is much smaller than the Sun but also much closer to Earth, allowing it to appear roughly the same size in our sky and cover the Sun's disk completely.\n\nThis alignment only occurs during a **New Moon**, but not *every* New Moon results in an eclipse. The Moon's orbit is tilted about 5 degrees relative to Earth's orbit around the Sun. Most of the time, during a New Moon, the Moon passes slightly above or below the Sun from our perspective. An eclipse only happens when the New Moon occurs near one of the two points where the orbits cross (called nodes).\n\n**Totality vs. Partial Eclipse:**\n- **Totality**: Occurs only in a narrow path on Earth where the Moon completely covers the Sun. Observers here see the Sun's corona and experience a brief period of darkness.\n- **Partial Eclipse**: Seen in a much wider area surrounding the path of totality, where the Moon covers only part of the Sun.\n\n**Source**:  \n[Total Solar Eclipse - Wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Total_solar_eclipse)\n\n**Does every New Moon cause a total eclipse?**  \nNo. Only about 2–4 solar eclipses occur each year, and even fewer are total. Most New Moons do not result in an eclipse because of the orbital tilt.",
      "sha256": "42bc9dd6d7244fdaa17d1d01a5ee9b7530f4ad048927c40d45d32298d1c75250"
    },
